  "category": "Presentation & Performance Control",
  "blurb": "Genigraphics began as a General Electric computer-graphics system for NASA space-flight simulation, and in 1973 GE delivered the first commercial 100 Series presentation system to Amoco. The console is the exhibit: an array of buttons, dials, knobs and joysticks alongside a built-in keyboard, all driving a PDP-11/40 through random-access DECtape to compose charts and titles that a Forox film recorder then burned onto 35mm or 72mm film at up to 2,000-line resolution. Later 100B–D+ models replaced the knob array with on-screen text menus, a graphics tablet and a pen; the pen gave way to a mouse-like device. In 1988 Microsoft and Genigraphics struck a deal: PowerPoint shipped with Genigraphics palettes and gradients and a 'Send to Genigraphics' command that modemmed your file to a bureau for overnight slide production. The room-sized knob console and the desktop template share one lineage, and the museum holds the origin point: a control surface so physical that a graphics operator worked it with both hands and a grid of joysticks.",
  "creator": "General Electric (Computed Images System & Services / CISS); spun off in 1982 as Genigraphics Corporation",
  "manufacturer": "General Electric / Genigraphics Corporation",
  "tagline": "Where corporate slides were made by hand, on knobs",
