  "notes": [
    "The 100 Series is the exhibit's strongest case of a *dedicated* graphics control surface: dozens of discrete physical controls, each bound to one transformation (a dial for size, a joystick for position), rather than a general-purpose mouse-and-menu UI. That is the pre-WIMP way of drawing with a computer.",
    "The interface lineage runs in reverse of the usual story: the 100B–D+ moved FROM knobs TO on-screen text menus plus a graphics tablet and pen, and then to a mouse-like device. The museum gets a rare chance to show a physical panel being replaced by a screen-based one within a single product family.",
    "PDP-11/40 backbone, DECtape random-access storage for completed presentations, Forox recorder at 2,000 lines (later 4,000 and 8,000 via duplex and 4x scanning). The output medium is film, not pixels — a screen-full-size slide for projection, not a saved file.",
    "The Last Starfighter's computer-generated scenes were calculated on a Cray X-MP and mastered with a Genigraphics film recorder — a nice cross-reference to the Digistar exhibit, whose prototype generated Star Trek II starfields.",
    "The PowerPoint connection is the payoff: PowerPoint 2.0 was designed for Genigraphics film recorders and shipped with Genigraphics color palettes, schemes, gradient backgrounds, and a 'Send to Genigraphics' menu item. PowerPoint 3.0's clip art, templates, sample decks, and chart style came from Genigraphics artists. This makes the knob console a direct genealogical ancestor of the default corporate slide.",
    "The business model is part of the interface: you did not print at your desk. You dialed a modem and an overnight courier did the rest. 'Presentation' as a mediated service rather than a local file operation."
  ]
